// Google Batch / Seqera Platform profile.
//
// WHY beforeScript SYMLINKS projectDir
//
// The pipeline invokes `pixi run --frozen --manifest-path ${projectDir}/pixi.toml ...`, which
// assumes the pinned pixi environment -- built once, at image-build time, via
// `pixi install && pixi run setup` -- lives on disk at whatever path Nextflow resolves for
// `${projectDir}` at runtime. On Sherlock that is true by construction: the repo is checked out
// once onto shared NFS (see conf/sherlock.config). On Google Batch, Nextflow instead stages the
// pipeline source fresh into a container-specific
// `/.nextflow/assets/.repos/<org>/<repo>/clones/<sha>` path that has nothing to do with where the
// image's baked environment actually lives (`/opt/pipeline`, per the Dockerfile used to build the
// image below). Without this fix, every task fails with `pixi: command not found` -- confirmed
// directly, 2026-08-26 -- because the projectDir pixi ever sees has no environment in it.
//
// beforeScript replaces the task's projectDir with a symlink to the image's baked copy before the
// task script runs, so `pixi run` finds the environment without the task needing its own network
// access or its own multi-minute `pixi install`. Two things about it are load-bearing:
//
//   - `${projectDir}` is not materialized inside the task container until something touches it --
//     Fusion mounts it lazily -- so `mkdir -p $(dirname ...)` forces that before the `ln`, or the
//     symlink silently fails and the task falls through to the same `pixi: command not found`.
//   - Every step is `|| true`. A `beforeScript` that itself errors aborts the task before the real
//     command runs, which reports as a bare "process terminated with an error exit status" with no
//     indication the failure was in setup rather than in the pipeline.
//
// DO NOT RUN THIS PROFILE LOCALLY. `${projectDir}` is only a throwaway per-task clone on Google
// Batch. Run locally (executor = local, the default outside a Batch task), it resolves to the
// actual checked-out repo, and `rm -rf ${projectDir}` deletes it for real -- this destroyed the
// working tree once already, while testing this exact file. To sanity-check this profile's config
// resolves correctly without running any process, use `nextflow config -profile gcb .`, never
// `-stub-run` or a real run, outside of an actual Google Batch task context.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO google{}/wave{}/fusion{} BLOCK HERE -- EXCEPT batch.spot
//
// Most of google{} comes from the Seqera Platform compute environment attached at launch
// (confirmed by diffing what was actually passed in at launch -- just `process.container` and
// `process.beforeScript` -- against the effective config Seqera reported back, which additionally
// carried `executor = 'google-batch'`, `google { project; location; batch { ... } }`,
// `wave.enabled`, `fusion.enabled` and `workDir`). Hard-coding those here would duplicate
// organization-specific config that belongs to the compute environment, not to this pipeline, and
// would go stale the moment the compute environment changes.
//
// `batch.spot` is different: it is a fact about the *workload*, not about the account it happens
// to run in, so it belongs in the pipeline's own config rather than only in one compute
// environment's settings. This pipeline already tolerates preemption without help -- every
// process's `errorStrategy` (conf/base.config) retries on exit 143, which is what a preempted
// Google Batch task returns, and this was exercised for real: three tasks were preempted and
// retried automatically during the 2026-08-26 production Nextflow-vs-sbatch comparison run
// (job 38978285; see docs/status.md), no intervention needed. Nothing here trades power estimates
// for cost -- a preempted task simply reruns -- so there is no reason to ever pay full price.
// Setting it here means a differently-configured compute environment can't silently make this
// pipeline run at on-demand prices; today's compute environment (`hgrm-cloud-batch-us-central-WIF`)
// already sets it too, so this is belt-and-suspenders, not a change in behaviour.
//
// THE CONTAINER IMAGE HAS NO DEFAULT, DELIBERATELY
//
// `wave.seqera.io/wt/503b3282d5b9/wave/build:8b888ed3bf47e1ea` is the image that this profile's
// design was validated against (full pipeline run, all 7 processes, 2026-08-26) -- but it was built
// via Wave's ephemeral (non-frozen) mode, which expires roughly 24h after the build completes. It
// is not safe to bake in as a default: a run months from now would fail on a stale/deleted image
// with no clue why. Build a durable replacement with Wave's `freeze: true` + a `buildRepository`
// (a container registry Seqera has credentials for) from the same recipe -- see
// docs/status.md's Nextflow section for the exact Dockerfile -- and pass the result via
// `--container_image`.

params {
    container_image = params.container_image ?: null
}

process {
    container    = params.container_image
    beforeScript = {
        "mkdir -p \$(dirname ${projectDir}) 2>&1 || true; " +
        "rm -rf ${projectDir} 2>&1 || true; " +
        "ln -sfn /opt/pipeline ${projectDir} 2>&1 || true"
    }
}

google {
    batch {
        spot = true
    }
}
